+# SELF-GUARD, and it must run before the `demo_common` import below.
+#
+# WHY HERE rather than in the caller. Hermit reports a CONSTANT mtime for every
+# file (measured: `stat -c %Y demos/lib/demo_common.py` gives the real mtime
+# natively and 1767225600 under `hermit run --strict`). CPython invalidates a
+# .pyc by comparing the source mtime it stats against the mtime recorded in the
+# .pyc header, so a hermited run and a native run sharing this directory
+# CONTINUOUSLY INVALIDATE EACH OTHER'S CACHE. The first hermited run after any
+# native one therefore recompiles `demo_common` and does measurably different
+# work, and only that one.
+#
+# That is not hypothetical. It made 2 of 10 runs of a QEMU boot experiment
+# diverge in their snapshots and was investigated for a day as a suspected
+# Detcore determinism hole before being traced here. See
+# `experiments/detcore-partial-determinism_20260819/README.md`.
+#
+# demos/05-qemu-boot.py and 06-qemu-resume.py already set
+# PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 in the child environment, so for them this is
+# belt-and-braces. The point of putting it HERE is that this module is the
+# single entry point every ad-hoc experiment driver executes inside Hermit, and
+# those drivers have no reason to know about the hazard. Guarding at the entry
+# point means they get it without knowing.
+#
+# `sys.dont_write_bytecode` is the in-process equivalent of the env var and,
+# unlike the env var, cannot be dropped by a caller that rebuilds the
+# environment from scratch.
+sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
+
